- Important filtering step to remove zero and low-count data:

#For the enriched data
RNA_sum <- RNA_sum %>% 
  filter(rowSums(across(ends_with("_CPM"), ~ .x > 0.5)) >= 3)
  
#For the unenriched data:
RNA_sum <- RNA_sum %>% 
  filter(rowSums(across(ends_with("_CPM"), ~ .x > 0.33)) >= 3)
  
The different threshold is due to differences in the sequencing depth for the two 
datasets. The sequencing depth for the enriched is only like 10'000'000, and the 
seqeuncing depth for the unenriched is like 65'000'000. I'm not sure how the original
authour landed on 0.33 and 0.5, but it appears to be arbitrary....

As the original authour wrote ""Zero and low-level count data was filtered out based on counts 
per million (CPM) to account for varying library sizes. Genes with > 0.33 CPM (~10 reads for 
a 30M library size) from the unenriched library and > 0.5 CPM (~3 reads for a 6M library size) 
for the enriched library in at least 3 of the 6 samples were retained."

From this it does not seem directly proportional to read depth (10/30M vs. 3/6M). Perhaps
we should take another look at this, but honestly it probably does not matter since it is 
only removing noise. 
     
